🎗️ 27.29 wait() vs sleep()

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
| `wait()`                            | `sleep()`                           |
| ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Releases monitor                    | Does not release monitor            |
| Used for thread communication       | Used for delaying execution         |
| Must be used with monitor ownership | Does not require synchronized block |
| Thread waits for notification       | Thread waits for specified time     |
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

👉Interview Answer
wait() releases the monitor while waiting, whereas sleep() keeps the monitor if the thread already owns it.

🎗️ 27.30 Busy Waiting vs wait()

--------------------------------------------------------
| Busy Waiting                  | `wait()`             |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------- |
| Continuously checks condition | Waits until notified |
| Wastes CPU                    | Efficient            |
| Does not release monitor      | Releases monitor     |
| Poor approach                 | Preferred approach   |
--------------------------------------------------------
👉 Bad

while(buffer.isEmpty()) {
}

👉 Correct

while(buffer.isEmpty()) {
    wait();
}

🎗️ 27.31 Common Mistakes
❌ Using if instead of while
❌ Calling wait() outside synchronized
❌ Forgetting notifyAll()
❌ Using different SharedBuffer objects
❌ Creating separate queues for Producer and Consumer
❌ Using busy waiting
❌ Forgetting the capacity condition
❌ Calling run() instead of start()

🎗️ 27.32 Best Practices
✔ Use one shared buffer
✔ Use one common monitor
✔ Always use while before wait()
✔ Use notifyAll() when multiple Producers/Consumers are possible
✔ Keep synchronization logic inside SharedBuffer
✔ Producer should not directly access the queue
✔ Consumer should not directly access the queue

🎗️ 27.33 Advantages
✔ Prevents race conditions
✔ Prevents buffer overflow
✔ Prevents buffer underflow
✔ Enables thread communication
✔ Avoids busy waiting
✔ Provides controlled access to shared data

🎗️ 27.34 Disadvantages
❌ More complex than basic synchronization
❌ Incorrect synchronization can cause deadlocks
❌ Incorrect notification can cause progress issues
❌ Multiple threads make debugging harder

